body:
Introduction
Adenosine deaminase acting on RNA 1 (ADAR1) is an RNA editing enzyme that hydrolytically deaminates adenosine to inosine in double-stranded RNA (dsRNA) [for review (
1
)]. Its catalytic activity is crucial for distinguishing between ’self’ and ‘non-self’ RNA, thus playing a critical role in innate immunity (
2
). ADAR1 exists in two isoforms; the constitutively expressed p110 isoform, mainly localized in the nucleolus (
3
), and the interferon (IFN)-inducible p150 isoform, which is predominantly cytoplasmic. Nuclear localization is determined by the nuclear localization signal located in the third dsRNA binding domain (dsRBD3) (
4
). The cytoplasmic presence of ADAR1 is established by the nuclear export signal in the Z
