domain (
6
). The cytoplasmic ADAR1p150 isoform is required to prevent aberrant innate immune responses [for review (
7
)].
The role of ADAR1 in innate immunity was elucidated with mouse genetic models. The
Adar
null mutant is embryonically lethal and dies by day E12.5 with hemopoietic defects liver disintegration, and a strong, aberrant IFN response (
8
,
9
). This lethality is rescued to live birth in
Adar Mavs
double mutants also lacking the Mavs (mitochondrial antiviral signaling) protein required for signaling from dsRNA-activated retinoic acid-inducible gene I (Rig-I)-like receptors (RLRs) (
2
,
10
,
11
). In another mouse model, a mutation in
Adar
that generates a catalytically inactive protein was combined with a mutation in the gene encoding melanoma differentiation-associated protein 5 (Mda5, gene name
Ifih1
), which is the RLR activated by long dsRNA (
12
). This double mutant completely rescues the
Adar
phenotype, is fertile, and has a normal lifespan. The differences between the
Adar
null and catalytically-inactive mutants demonstrate that the Adar1 protein itself also has biological functions independent of its RNA editing activity. Recently it was shown that a triple mutant combining an allele of
Adar
mutated to generate only the p110 isoform with mutations in
Ifih1
and in the gene encoding Pkr (
Eif2ka2
), rescues the
Adar
phenotype independent of the editing activity (
13
,
14
). While the phenotypes of
Adar
mutant mice are consistent for each particular allele, the effect of ADAR1 depletion in cultured cells varies. In some cell lines,
ADAR
deletion results in cell death, whereas in others, cells survive without it (
15
); cell death is rescued by depleting MDA5 or, in some cell types, by additional depletion of PKR.
Given the importance of the ADAR1 editing-independent function and the variations in ADAR1’s interactions and effects across different cells, we decided to explore ADAR1 protein interactions with three different enrichment methods combined with liquid chromatography-tandem mass spectrometry (LC–MS/MS). This was performed in human cell lines so we could compare our results which those previously published on the ADAR1 interactome (
16
,
17
). First, we mapped the interactome of endogenous ADAR1 in human A549 cells previously used to study ADAR1 (
15
), in the presence and absence of RNase A treatment. To discern isoform-specific interactions, we created stable cell lines with the Flp-In™ T-REx™ 293 cell line, expressing either ADAR1p110, ADAR1p150 or mutant ADAR1 isoforms incapable of dsRNA binding (EAA). We utilized the Strep II-HA affinity tag to study stable interactions of individual ADAR1 isoforms, with both data-dependent acquisition (DDA) and data-independent acquisition (DIA) modes of MS/MS. To capture spatial and temporal interactions, we employed BioID, tagging ADAR1 with biotin ligase BirA* which labels proteins within an approximate radius of 10 nm. Lastly, since ADAR1p150 expression is normally induced by IFN (
18
) we also examined the ADAR1 isoform interactomes under IFN stimulation with IFNα or HMW poly (I:C) (henceforth referred to as p(I:C)) treatments.
With these different methods (
19
), we have identified both known and novel ADAR1 interactors. ADAR1 engages in a broad range of interactions, aligning with its known biological roles. IFN stimulation expands the ADAR1 protein interactome to include viral replication inhibitors and L1 regulators. Analysis of the proximal protein network reveals that after IFN and p(I:C) treatments, the ADAR1 interactome changes: ADAR1p110 interacts with nuclear export machinery and dsRNA binding proteins (dsRBPs), while ADAR1p150 associates with antiviral proteins and with cytoplasmic condensates. Mutations affecting dsRNA binding alter interactions differently depending on the ADAR1 isoforms, notably impacting ADAR1p110’s nucleolar interactions.
Materials and methods
Cells, culture conditions and treatments
Human Flp-In™ T-REx™ 293 cell lines (Invitrogen) were maintained in Dulbecco’s modified Eagle’s medium DMEM) Low (# LM-D1102/500, Biosera), supplemented with 10% tetracycline-free fetal bovine serum (FBS) (#P30-3602, Biosera). A549 cells were maintained in DMEM (# LM-D1110/500, Biosera) supplemented with 10% FBS (#FB-1001B/500, Biosera) and 1% non-essential amino acids (#M7145-100M, Sigma–Aldrich). To each culture media 1% penicillin–streptomycin solution (#XC-A4122/100, Biosera) was added.
For induction of the IFN response, cells were treated with 2000 IU/ml of human IFNα 2a (#SRP4594, Merck) or 0.3 or 0.5 μg/ml of poly(I:C) (HMW) / LyoVec™ (#tlrl-piclv, InvivoGen). For the RNase A experiments, cells were treated with 2000 IU/ml of Human IFN-alpha 2 (alpha2a) recombinant protein (# 300–02AA-100UG, Thermo Fisher Scientific). The specific time points and concentration used for each experiment are described in the figure legends and main text.
Preparation of expression constructs and stable cells
Coding sequences for ADAR1p150 and ADAR1p110, both wild-type (WT) and EAA mutant, were amplified with Phusion™ High-Fidelity DNA Polymerase (#F530L, Thermo Scientific), following the manufacturer’s instructions with gene-specific primers (
Supplementary Table S5
) from Generi Biotech. Gateway™ recombination technology (Thermo Fisher) was used to generate the constructs. The vectors were pTO-Strep II-H (
20
) and pTO-BirA*-MYC and the ADAR1 inserts encoded N-terminal epitope tags. The ADAR1p110 EAA mutant was a kind gift from Kazuko Nishikura (
21
).
Flp-In™ T-REx™ 293 cell line (Invitrogen) was co-transfected with 300 ng of the
ADAR
plasmid (pTO-Strep II-H or pTO-BirA*-MYC) and 2.7 μg of pOG44 vector for Flp-recombinase expression (Invitrogen) with TurboFect™ Transfection Reagent (#R0532, Thermo Scientific) or Lipofectamine™ 3000 Transfection Reagent (#L3000015, Invitrogen) as per the manufacturer’s instructions. After 24 h, cells were transferred to a 150 mm cell culture dish and selected for 2–3 weeks with 100 μg/ml of hygromycin B Gold (#ant-hg-1, Invivogen). Individual clones were screened by induction with 1 μg/ml of doxycycline for 24 h followed by immunoblotting. Positive clones were further expanded and used. Generation of the Flp-In™ T-REx™ 293 cell line expressing the green fluorescent protein (GFP) control (BirA*-MYC or Strep II-HA) was previously described (
22
).
RNA extraction, cDNA synthesis, and quantitative PCR
Total RNA was isolated with TriPure reagent (#T9424, Merck) following the manufacturer’s instructions and treated with 2 U of TURBO™ DNase (#AM2239, Invitrogen) for 20–30 min at 37°C in the presence of RiboLock RNase Inhibitor (#EO0382, Thermo Scientific). DNase-treated total RNA was purified with phenol-chloroform extraction and an ethanol precipitation with sodium acetate. Precipitated RNA (0.5 μg) was then reverse transcribed with RevertAid Reverse Transcriptase kit (#EP0442, Thermo Scientific) and random hexamers, following the protocol for first strand cDNA synthesis on the manufacturer’s website. Quantitative-PCR was performed on a LightCycler
®
480 Instrument II (Roche) with LightCycler
®
480 SYBR Green I Master (#04 887 352 001, Roche) with the following cycling conditions: 95°C for 5 min; followed by 40 cycles of 95°C for 10 s; 56°C for 20 s; 72°C for 10 s; and 40°C for 10 s. Each reaction contained 10 ng of cDNA and gene-specific primers (
Supplementary Table S5
). Samples were analyzed with three biological and three technical replicates and transcripts were normalized to two housekeeping transcripts (
GAPDH
and
RPL13
). For relative quantification of gene expression the comparative CT method (ΔΔCT) was used (
23
). Statistical analysis was performed with a two-sided, unpaired t-test in the R programming language (
24
,
25
) for mean comparisons between IFNα and non-treated (NT) samples.
Immunofluorescence
Cells were seeded on coverslips coated with 0.1% gelatin in 24-well plates. Following treatment, the cells were fixed with 4% paraformaldehyde for 20 min at room temperature (RT) and permeabilized with 0.2% Triton X-100 for 20 min. Blocking was performed with 5% bovine serum albumin for 1 h at 37°C. The coverslips were then incubated with primary antibodies (
Supplementary Table S6
) in a humid chamber at 4°C overnight. Afterwards, the coverslips were washed thrice with phosphate-buffered saline (PBS) and incubated with secondary antibodies (Alexa Fluor 488 goat anti-mouse IgG (H + L): Thermo Fisher Scientific, cat. A-11001, 1:500, Alexa Fluor 594 goat anti-rabbit IgG (H + L): Thermo Fisher Scientific, cat. A-11012, 1:500) for 1 h at 37°C. Subsequently, they were washed three times with PBS, once with distilled water, and mounted on slides (Superfrost Plus, Thermo Fisher Scientific) using ProLong Gold Antifade reagent with DAPI (Thermo Fisher Scientific). The slides were left to solidify overnight in the dark. The samples were visualized with a Zeiss LSM 780 confocal microscope with a 63× (1.4 numerical aperture) oil-immersion objective lens. The raw images were processed in Fiji (
26
).
Immunoblotting of total cell lysate
Samples were lysed for 30 min in lysis buffer [0.5% NP40; 50 mM 4-(2-hydroxyethyl)-1-piperazineethanesulfonicacid (HEPES), pH 8.0; 5 mM ethylenediaminetetraacetic acid (EDTA); 150 mM NaCl] supplemented with 1× Halt™ Protease Inhibitor Cocktail, EDTA-Free (#78 439, Thermo Fisher) and 1× Halt™ Phosphatase Inhibitor Cocktail (#78 427, Thermo Fisher) unless specified otherwise. Lysis was followed by centrifugation at 18 000
g
for 20 min and total protein quantification was performed with the Bicinchoninic acid assay (BCA) Protein Assay Kit (#23 225, Pierce™). Samples were denatured at 95°C for 5 min in Laemmli Sample Buffer and loaded on Tris-HCl sodium dodecyl sulphate-polyacrylamide gel electrophoresis (SDS-PAGE) or a mPAGE
®
4–12% Bis-Tris Precast Gel (#MP41G10, Merk). Separated proteins were transferred to a nitrocellulose membrane (#10 600 003, VWR) with a Mini Trans-Blot
®
Cell (Bio-Rad) for 1 h at 100 V. Membranes were blocked and subsequently incubated with primary antibodies overnight at 4°C, and with secondary antibodies for 1 h at RT. Antibody information is provided in
Supplementary Table S6
. Clarity Western ECL Substrate (#1 705 061, Bio-Rad) and UVITEC ChemiDoc imaging device were used for detection.
Endogenous co-immunoprecipitation and RNase A treatment
Following 30 h of treatment with 2000 IU/ml of IFNα2a or 0.5 μg/ml of poly(I:C), A549 cells were processed immediately or snap-frozen in liquid nitrogen and stored at −80°C. Lysis was performed for 30 min in lysis buffer (50 mM Tris–HCl, pH 8.0; 150 mM NaCl; 0.5% NP40 and 1 mM EDTA, pH 8.0, supplemented with protease cOmplete™ Protease Inhibitor Cocktail (#11 697 498 001, Sigma). For the RNase A experiment, EDTA was omitted from the lysis buffer. Equal amounts of proteins, quantified with the BCA Protein Assay Kit (#23 225, Pierce™), were used for subsequent steps of IPs and part was set aside for ‘input’ sample. For the RNase A digestion experiment, 30 μg/ml of RNase A (Thermo, EN0531) was added to 3 mg of total protein and incubated for 1 h at 4°C. Samples without the RNase A digestion, were incubated with 30 U/ml of RiboLock RNase Inhibitor (Thermo Scientific™, EO0381). Samples for the endo-IP experiment I and II were pre-cleared with Dynabeads
TM
Protein G (#10003D, Thermo Fisher) for 1 h and subsequently incubated for an additional hour with ADAR1 (#ABIN2855100, Antibodies.com) or IgG control (#30000–0-AP, Proteintech) antibodies. Equilibrated beads (Dynabeads
TM
Protein G) were added to the lysate-antibody solution and incubated overnight on a rotation shaker at 4°C. For the RNase A endo-IP experiment, digested or undigested sample lysate was incubated with ADAR1 or the IgG control antibody overnight and equilibrated Dynabeads
TM
Protein G were added next day for 1 h. For the RNase A experiment, part of the sample was taken for RNA extraction before adding beads. Following the indicated incubations, the beads were washed 6× with the lysis buffer prepared for LC–MS/MS analysis. For the RNase A experiments beads were washed 3× with lysis buffer and 3× with wash buffer (50 mM Tris–HCl, pH 8.0; 200 mM NaCl; and 1 mM EDTA, pH 8.0). Part of beads (∼10%) were retained and analyzed by SDS-PAGE electrophoresis and immunoblotting as described above. RNase A validation experiments were performed as described above, with slight modifications; 2.8 mg/ml of total protein was incubated with RNase A (10 μg/ml) or RiboLock (20 U/ml). Following 1 h of incubation, RiboLock was added to the RNase A sample and endo-IP steps were as described above.
Strep II-tag co-immunoprecipitation
Flp-In™ T-REx™ 293 cell line with stably integrated Strep II-HA-N-tagged ADAR1 and GFP, were induced with doxycycline (1 μg/ml for 28 h) and subsequently treated with IFNα (2000 IU/ml for 24 h). Cells were lysed in 1 ml of lysis buffer [50 mM HEPES, pH 8.0; 5 mM EDTA; 150 mM NaCl; 0.5% NP40; 1 mM dithiothreitol (DTT) and 2× Halt™ Protease Inhibitor Cocktail, EDTA-Free (#78 439, Thermo Fisher)] for 40 min. After centrifuged at 12 000 rpm for 20 min, part of the supernatant was retained as an ‘input’ sample and remainder was incubated with 55 μl of equilibrated MagStrep ‘type3’ XT beads (#2–4090-002, IBA GmbH), overnight on a rotation shaker at 4°C. The next day, after six washes with the lysis buffer, part of the beads (10%) was used for immunoblot analysis (as described above) and the remaining volume was prepared for LC–MS/MS analysis.
Proximity-dependent biotin identification labeling and purification
Flp-In™ T-REx™ 293 cell line stably expressing pTO-BirA*-MYC-N-tagged ADAR1 or GFP were grown in 100 mm cell culture dishes (two per each condition). For induction, 1 μg/ml of doxycycline was added for a total of 28 h; 4 h after doxycycline induction, 50 μM of biotin (#B4501, Sigma) and IFNα2a (2000 IU/ml) were added to the cells. The next day, 4 h before cell collection, cells were treated with 0.5 μg/ml HMW poly (I:C)/LyoVec™ (#tlrl-piclv, InvivoGen). The cells were pelleted and three washes with PBS was performed and if not processed immediately after pelleting, the samples were flash-frozen and stored at −80°C. Affinity purification of biotinylated protein was performed as described in (
27
) with some minor modifications as described (
28
). The sonication step was performed with the same setting and time as described but with a 5/64 probe and QSonica, Q55A instrument. The cleared lysate was incubated overnight with 85 μl of magnetic streptavidin beads (#65 001, Thermo Fisher) on a rotating wheel at 4°C. Part of the beads was retained for immunoblotting analysis with HRP-conjugated streptavidin (
Supplementary Table S6
) as described (
27
) and the remainder prepared for LC–MS/MS analysis.
Sample preparation and LC–MS/MS analysis
Full details regarding the analyses and data evaluation are in Supplemental Materials. In summary, for BioID and RNase A endo-IP analysis, the bead-bound protein complexes were treated with AMBIC and trypsin, incubated at 37°C for 2 h, followed by reduction with Tris(2-carboxyethyl)phosphinehydrochloride (TCEP) and alkylation with Iodoacetamide (IAA). After an additional 16 h of trypsin incubation at 37°C, peptides were quenched with TFA, desalted with C18 MicroSpin columns and reconstituted in [0.1% (v/v) trifluoroaceticacid (TFA) and 1% (v/v) acetonitrile (#83640.320, VWR)] in high pressure liquid chromatography (HPLC) water. Samples were further diluted with HPLC water containing formic acid and loaded onto an Evotips. They were analyzed with the Evosep One liquid chromatography system coupled to a hybrid trapped ion mobility quadrupole Time-of-flight (TOF) mass spectrometer (Bruker timsTOF Pro, Bruker Daltonics) (
29
) via a CaptiveSpray nano-electrospray ion source (Bruker Daltonics).
For Strep II-tag IP analysis, the bead-bound protein complexes were mixed with 2% SDS solution and heated to 50°C for 20 min after which the proteins were reduced with DTT at 95°C for 15 min. The resulting protein solutions were subjected to filter-aided sample preparation as previously described (
30
). The resulting peptides were analyzed by LC–MS/MS performed with the UltiMate 3000 RSLCnano system (Thermo Fisher Scientific) on-line coupled with Orbitrap Fusion Lumos spectrometer (Thermo Fisher Scientific).
High-confidence interactors and label-free quantification
High-confidence interactors (HCIs) for endo-IP and BioID experiments were defined as previously described (
31
). In summary, cut-off criteria for HCIs with SAINT (
32
) included Bayesian false discovery rate (BFDR) <0.05, Average spectral count ≥4, Fold Change >4, SAINT score >0.7 and <20% for CRAPome (
33
). If a protein presence in CRAPome was >20%, the average spectral count fold change limit was set to 3 or manually checked when spectral counts for bait (ADAR1) differed between conditions. For the Strep II-tag IP experiment, differential expression with the LIMMA statistical test was used, with the following criteria: log
2
FC relative to GFP >2; adj.
P
-value <0.01; average number of peptides ≥4 present in at least two biological replicates. Strep II-tag DDA dataset was further filtered against the CRAPome, proteins not passing criteria described above were removed from both DDA and DIA dataset. Ribosomal and cytoskeletal proteins that passed the criteria described above were removed manually before further analyses.
For data pre-processing an in-house python and R-scripts were used. Raw intensity values were log
2
transformed and median-normalized. Afterward, missing values were imputed with QRILC imputation (
34
). For sample group comparison, log
2
FC were obtained by the ratio of the bait-normalized means and the
P
-values were calculated with Student’s
t
-test. These values were used for generating the volcano plots with an in-house R-script. For ADAR1-normalized spectral counts, the average spectral counts for each prey was normalized to the average ADAR1 spectral counts for comparisons.
The Gene Ontology (GO)-terms analysis, IFN-related proteins and protein network visualization
GO-terms analysis was performed with The Database for Annotation, Visualization and Integrated Discovery (DAVID, version: v2024q2) (
35
). The background was set to ‘Homo sapiens’ and the ‘annotation chart’ option was chosen. Terms were filtered based on Bonferroni, Benjamini and false discovery rate (FDR) values, all set to be <0.01. A full list of the enriched terms and relevant statistical values for the BioID experiment are available in the
Supplementary Table S2
.
IFN-related proteins were filtered with the Interferome database [version 2.01 (
36
)]. HCIs were searched for the term ‘Homo sapiens’ and the fold change was set to >2. Protein networks were constructed with STRING database version: 12.0 (
37
,
38
), both physical interactions and functional interactions with 0.4 confidence. Networks were visualized with Cytoscape [version 3.9.1 (
39
)].
Statistical analyses, visualization of the data
All graphical data visualization in this manuscript was generated with RStudio (v. 2022.07.2 + 576 with R v. 4.2.2) and the ggplot2 R package (
40
). Graphical abstract and schemes displayed in Figures
1A
,
2A
,
3A
and
4A
,
Supplementary Figure S1A
were created with BioRender.
Figure 1.
Interactomes of ADAR1 isoforms during homeostasis. (
A
) Scheme depicting the experimental approach used for the identification of stable HCIs of ADAR1. Endogenous ADAR1 and associated proteins were immunoprecipitated (IP) with an ADAR1-specific antibody and subsequently analyzed by LC–MS/MS in the DDA mode. Doxycycline-inducible stable cell lines 293 T-REx expressing either ADAR1p110 (yellow) or ADAR1p150 (green) were generated, enabling isoform-specific Strep-tag IP. The bound proteins were subjected to LC–MS/MS in both DDA and DIA modes. This image was created with BioRender. (
B
) Venn diagram illustrating the overlaps between the HCIs of endogenous ADAR1 and Strep-tagged ADAR1 isoforms, with both DDA and DIA modes. (
C
) Functional protein network of HCIs identified for both endogenous ADAR1 and Strep-tagged ADAR1 isoforms [areas highlighted in color in panel (B)]. The network was generated in STRING with medium confidence and visualized with Cytoscape. Node colors represent isoform-specific interactors (grey– both ADAR1 isoforms, yellow – ADAR1p110 unique, dark green - ADAR1p150 unique, light green - ADAR1p110 unique with DDA and both ADAR1 isoforms with DIA), outline colors indicate the experiment in which the HCIs were identified and the thicknesses of the interconnected lines denote the combined scores for particular interactions. The clustering annotation was based on an enrichment analysis with the STRING app in Cytoscape.
Figure 2.
Distinct proximal protein networks of ADAR1 isoforms during homeostasis. (
A
) Scheme depicting the experimental approach used for the identification of proteins proximal to ADAR1. Doxycycline-inducible stable cell lines 293 T-REx expressing either ADAR1p110 (yellow) or ADAR1p150 (green) with BirA* at the N-terminus were generated, enabling isoform-specific proximity labeling. The biotinylated proteins bound to the beads were subjected to LC–MS/MS in the DDA mode. The image was created with BioRender. (
B
) Venn diagram depicting the extent of overlap between the high-confidence neighboring proteins for both ADAR1 isoforms identified with BioID. (
C
) Gene ontology (GO) terms of the cellular components significantly enriched for the proximal protein network of ADAR1p110 and ADAR1p150. The height of the bar corresponds to the fold enrichment and its color to the ADAR1 isoform. Small subunit (SSU) processome, cytoplasmic ribonucleoprotein granules (c. RNP granules), P-bodies (PBs), cytoplasmic SGs (c. SGs), coding region instability determinant (CRD)-mediated mRNA stability complex (CRD-complex). (
D
) Functional protein network of high-confidence proximal proteins identified with BioID for both ADAR1 isoforms. Node colors correspond to the ADAR1 isoforms to which the protein is proximal (red– not present, light red – decreased, grey – unchanged, blue - increased), line thickness indicates combined String score and the node size corresponds to bait-normalized spectral counts of the identified proximal protein. The clustering annotation is based on the enrichment analysis with the STRING app in Cytoscape and the additional comparison to a published dataset was performed for SGs and PBs.
Figure 3.
Effect of RNase A digestion on ADAR1 protein interactions. (
A
) Scheme depicting the experimental approach used for the identification of endogenous ADAR1 interactors occurring on long RNA. A549 cell lysates were incubated with RNase A or RNase inhibitor for 1 h at 4°C before adding either ADAR1 or IgG antibodies for subsequent overnight incubation. The image was created with BioRender. (
B
) Number of ADAR1 HCIs identified after SAINT filtering of co-IP samples with and without RNase A treatment. (
C
) The physical protein interaction network of HCIs identified in the RNase experiment and the two endogenous ADAR1 co-IP experiments. Node colors represent qualitative comparisons of bait-normalized spectral counts of HCIs between samples with and without RNase A treatment. Line thickness indicates combined Saint score, and the node size corresponds to bait-normalized spectral counts of the identified interactor.
Figure 4.
Effect of ADAR1 EAA mutants on protein interactions and networks. (
A
) Scheme depicting experimental approaches used for the identification of stable interactors and proximal protein networks occurring on dsRNA. Doxycycline-inducible stable cell lines (293 T-REx) expressing either ADAR1p110 (yellow) or ADAR1p150 (green) as well as ADAR1p110 EAA and ADAR1p150 EAA isoforms were generated. Both Strep-tag IP and BioID were performed and the bound proteins were subjected to LC–MS/MS analyses. The image was created with BioRender. (
B
) Number of HCIs or high-confidence proximal proteins identified with the WT and ADAR1 EAA mutants of both ADAR1 isoforms with the different methods. The color of the bar corresponds to the ADAR1 isoform (yellow– ADAR1p110 and green – ADAR1p150). (
C
) Dot plot of ADAR1 interactors or proximal proteins identified as showing changes with RNase A treatment that overlap in the two different experiments (Strep-tag IP and BioID) for both ADAR1 isoforms and the effect of the ADAR1 EAA mutant. The size of the dot corresponds to bait-normalized average spectra of the identified protein and color corresponds to the ADAR1 isoform (yellow– ADAR1p110 and green – ADAR1p150). The blue line beside the gene name of some proteins, marks those with nucleolar localization. (
D
) Dot plot of ADAR1 interactors or proximal proteins that are identified as showing no changes after RNase A treatment that overlap in the Strep-tag IP and BioID experiments for both ADAR1 isoforms and the effect of the ADAR1 EAA mutant compared to WT protein. The size of the dot corresponds to bait-normalized average spectra of the identified protein and color to ADAR1 isoform (yellow– ADAR1p110 and green – ADAR1p150). Blue line marks the proteins with nucleolar localization.
Results
The ADAR1 interactome in homeostasis comprises proteins with roles in RNA metabolism
To identify stable high-affinity interactions of ADAR1, endogenous ADAR1 was co-immunoprecipitated (endo-IP) from A549 lung carcinoma epithelial cells (Figure
1A
and
Supplementary Figure S1A
and
B
). However, with this approach, it is not possible to differentiate between interactions formed with the nuclear ADAR1p110 and those with the cytoplasmic ADAR1p150 isoform. To address this limitation, inducible Flp-In™ T-REx™ 293 cell lines (henceforth referred to as 293 T-REx) were generated to separately express different ADAR1 isoforms; ADAR1p150 or ADAR1p110, with Strep II-HA tags at the N-termini for Strep-tag IPs (Figure
1A
and
Supplementary Figure S1C
) (
41
,
42
). The proteins enriched following Strep-tag IPs were subjected to two distinct MS/MS data acquisitions. The DDA method detects the most abundant sample components while the DIA method provides a more comprehensive coverage of interacting proteins, particularly the low-abundance components in the sample (
43
,
44
). The HCIs were determined with SAINT and CRAPome filtering for the endo-IP and LIMMA filtering cut-off was used for Strep-tag experiments, as described in the Materials and Methods section. The number of HCIs was comparable between the two endo-IP experiments and the Strep-tag IP with DIA, while the number of HCIs were lower for the DDA dataset (
Supplementary Figure S1D
and
Supplementary Table S1
). As the two endogenous ADAR1 co-IP experiments exhibited some distinct HCIs, we focused our subsequent analyses only on those that were common to both (
Supplementary Table S1
).
A comparison of the endogenous ADAR1 co-IP and Strep-tag IP HCI datasets for both ADAR1 isoforms revealed that 9 and 37 common interactors for DDA and DIA respectively. (Figure
1B
). An additional 11 HCIs of endogenous ADAR1 were identified as ADAR1p110-unique with DDA, while using DIA slightly increased the number to 17. A significant overlap in HCIs was observed between the two ADAR1 isoforms with DDA, with unique proteins interacting only with ADAR1p110 (Figure
1B
). A similar overlapping pattern was observed with DIA, although this acquisition technique identified a relatively small number (21 in total) of ADAR1p150-unique interactors. Furthermore, an overlap in the interactomes of both isoforms is also evident at the MS1 or DIA intensity levels. (
Supplementary Figure S1E
). These results indicate that, in homeostasis, ADAR1p150 forms relatively few stable interactions, the majority of which are also observed with ADAR1p110, implying that they might represent interactions of the small fraction of ADAR1p150 protein localized in the nucleus (
5
,
45
).
The HCIs identified as common to endogenous ADAR1 and Strep-tagged ADAR1 isoforms are likely to represent true stable, high-affinity ADAR1 interactors and potential regulators of A-to-I RNA editing, irrespective of cell type. The functional protein network of these common HCIs revealed three main clusters (Figure
1C
). These clusters include proteins involved in rRNA biogenesis and processing, mRNA processing and a mixed cluster of endoribonuclease complexes and deadenylation-dependent mRNA decay proteins. The majority of proteins were ADAR1p110 interactors or were found with both ADAR1 isoforms. While SRRM2, MAP7, ZC3HAV1 and LARP1 interact solely with ADAR1p150. The majority of HCIs displayed in the network are localized in the nuclear and nucleolar compartments and their predominant identification is as HCIs of ADAR1p110. (
Supplementary Figure S1F
).
In summary, ADAR1, in homeostasis, interacts with proteins involved in RNA metabolism, mainly related to rRNA biogenesis and processing and mRNA processing. Moreover, unique interactions are specific to the ADAR1p110 isoform, while ADAR1p150 shares some common interactors but forms relatively few stable interactions.
Delineating proximal protein networks of ADAR1 isoforms with BioID
Both endo-IP and Strep-tag IP methods revealed stable and high affinity interactors which were predominantly localized in the nucleolus and the nucleus. The majority of the interactors were found with both ADAR1 isoforms or were unique to the ADAR1p110 isoform, suggesting that ADAR1p150 interactions in the cytoplasm are more transient. To further address this question and identify proteins that transiently interact with ADAR1 we have utilized BioID, a proximity labeling method (
46
). Two ADAR1 isoforms were tagged with BirA* at the N-terminus and individual cell lines were created (Figure
2A
). Following enrichment of biotinylated proteins (
Supplementary Figure S2A
) LC–MS/MS analysis in the DDA mode was performed. In contrast to Strep-tag IPs, the number of high-confidence proximal proteins (term used to describe the proteins identified with BioID), was slightly higher but comparable for both isoforms (
Supplementary Figure S2B
and
Supplementary Table S2
). Both ADAR1 isoforms displayed distinct proximal protein networks with 6 proteins being proximal to both isoforms (Figure
2B
). These included, besides ADAR1, ILF3 (
47
,
48
), ZFR (
48
), DHX9 (
49–51
), known interactors of ADAR1, as well as STAU2 and RBM33.
The GO-term enrichment analyses emphasize the differences in the proximal protein networks between ADARp110 and ADAR1p150 (Figures
2C
,
Supplementary Figure S2C
, and
Supplementary Table S2
). ADAR1p110 proximal proteins are primarily found in the nucleoplasm, on the chromosomes, and in the nucleolus (SSU processome and 90S preribosome). In contrast, proteins interacting with ADAR1p150 are enriched for cytoplasm, cytoplasmic RNP granules, PBs, cytoplasmic SGs and CRD-mediated mRNA stability complex (CRD-complex).
The individual high-confidence proximal proteins identified for each isoform were used to generate a functional protein network (Figure
2D
). A clear distinction was observed between the ADAR1 isoform protein clusters, with few proteins being common. However, these proteins show different relative abundances, as indicated by the node sizes. Of these, ILF3, ZFR and DHX9 are more enriched with ADAR1p110 while STAU2 and RBM33 are similar between the two isoforms. Other high-confidence proximal proteins clustered in three different main clusters. Those proximal to ADAR1p110 included proteins with roles in rRNA processing and RNA helicase activity, a cluster also observed for stable interactions (Figure
1C
). Also proximal to ADAR1p110 were components associated with the U2-type spliceosomal complex and mRNA splicing. The components of these two main clusters are in agreement with the localization of ADAR1p110 (
3
,
4
,
45
). The ADAR1p150 proximal protein network on the other hand was more heterogeneous. Considering that GO-terms enriched with ADAR1p150 are related to cellular condensates (Figure
2C
), we compared the BioID dataset with the dataset of SGs and PBs components (
52
). The majority of ADAR1p150 proximal proteins are described as a component of SGs, PBs, or of both (Figure
2D
,
Supplementary Figure S2D
and
Supplementary Table S2
). Considering that our cells were unstressed, finding significant overlap with these condensates indicates that ADAR1p150 could be a part of the core proteins required for the rapid formation of the condensates during stress (
52
,
53
). Moreover, recent profiling of RBPs binding dynamics during the mRNA life cycle uncovered that the SGs proteome is enriched on aged mRNAs (
54
). Therefore, we compared the proximal protein networks of both ADAR1 isoforms with this dataset. ADAR1p110 proximal RBPs display nuclear mRNA binding dynamic, while ADAR1p150 proximal RBPs have a broader range suggesting cytosolic mRNA binding (
Supplementary Figure S2E
and
Supplementary Table S2
). Moreover, the ADAR1p110 proximal proteins are found to belong to clusters II, III, and IV and ADAR1p150 proximal proteins to VI and VII (
Supplementary Figure S2F
). Clusters II, III and IV were enriched for pre-mRNA splicing, nucleocytoplasmic transport and rRNA processing while clusters VI and VII were enriched for translation regulation, SG, and mRNA stability. These terms were taken from the original publication (
54
).
Lastly, we find a certain overlap of the BioID dataset with those we identified as stable interactor for both isoforms, with more proteins in common with the ADAR1p110 isoform (
Supplementary Figure S2G
and
Supplementary Table S2
). Three proteins are identified regardless of the method used including ILF3 which was present with both ADAR1 isoforms, while KIF23 and IGFBP1 were found with ADAR1p110 and ADAR1p150, respectively (
Supplementary Figure S2H
). Moreover, proximal proteins identified with ADAR1p110 show more overlap with the Strep-tag dataset while those identified with ADAR1p150 have a greater overlap with the endo-IP dataset.
In summary, proximity labeling allows us to distinguish distinct ADAR1 isoform-specific proximal protein networks not observed with other methods. The protein networks and associated biological roles match the predominant localization of each isoform. The proximity of ADAR1p150 to core components of cellular condensates and to ‘aged’ mRNPs was also revealed.
Impact of RNase treatment on endogenous ADAR1 interactions
As ADAR1 is a dsRNA-binding protein, performing co-IP can enrich for interactors that occur on longer RNA molecules. To determine which of the identified HCIs of endogenous ADAR1 are bridged by RNA molecules, the A549 cell lysate was incubated with RNase A or left untreated prior to co-IP with endogenous ADAR1 and LC–MS/MS analysis (Figure
3A
).
RNase A treatment did not affect the immunoprecipitation of ADAR1 (
Supplementary Figure S3A
) but it resulted in significant RNA degradation (
Supplementary Figure S3B
). RNase A treatment resulted in fewer HCIs (Figure
3B
and
Supplementary Table S3
) even though the total number of identified proteins was higher (
Supplementary Figure S3C
). Importantly, for this endo-IP experiment, we defined HCIs utilizing SAINT filtering while omitting CRApome filtering, in contrast to the other two endo-IP experiments described in Figure
1
. This was due to differences in ADAR1 spectral counts in the three experiments which can affect CRAPome filtering. Nevertheless, we identified high number of proteins overlapping between the RNase A and the two endo-IP experiments (
Supplementary Table S3
).
These overlapping HCIs were used to generate a protein network of physical interactions which revealed four main clusters (Figure
3C
). Further comparison of ADAR1-normalized spectral counts of HCIs between samples with and without RNase A treatment revealed the effect of RNase A treatment on HCIs (Figure
3C
). The more distal interactors of endogenous ADAR1 include proteins from all clusters; for example, various heterogeneous nuclear RNPs (hnRNPs) as well as some proteins with roles in ribosomal processing. However, not all hnRNPs seem to be distal interactors. A significant number of HCIs were unaffected and a few were even increased with RNase A digestion, indicating that these are likely to comprise of a group of close interactors. We have validated some of the interactions and the effect of RNase A treatment with endo-IP and immunoblotting (
Supplementary Figure S3D
). Quantification of the signal intensity agrees with results obtained with the LC–MS/MS data for all tested proteins except for NOP2, which was not significantly depleted with RNase A as determined with immunoblotting (
Supplementary Figure S3E
). Of HCIs unaffected by the RNase A treatment we identified some of the dsRBDs, including ILF2, ILF3, DHX9, STAU1, STAU2, TARBP2, PKR (EIF2AK2) and PRKRA, of which the majority are known ADAR1 interactors. While PKR, ILF3, and ILF2 were demonstrated to be ADAR1 interactors bridged by RNA molecules (
16
,
47
); conflicting results are found in the literature for DHX9 (
17
,
49
). Quantification of the signal obtained with endogenous ADAR1 co-IP and immunoblotting revealed a high degree of variability for some interactors with RNase A treatment (
Supplementary Figure S3D
and
E
). This could be also attributed to the incomplete digestion of RNA by RNase A and the presence of remaining RNA fragments (
Supplementary Figure S3B
and
F
). The presence of the RNA fragments which proteins may bind to, could lead to the false identification of the interactor as RNA-independent.
By using RNase A digestion prior to endogenous ADAR1 co-IP and subsequent LC–MS/MS analysis, we identified proximal and distal interactomes of ADAR1 in A549 cells. Subsequent co-IP and immunoblotting experiments are in agreement with LC–MS/MS data, but they also revealed high variability. Importantly, some proximal interactions might occur on the remaining RNA fragments following the RNase A digestion.
Effect of ADAR1 dsRNA-binding mutants on protein interactions and proximal protein networks
An ADAR1 dsRNA-binding mutant was used to elucidate whether the protein interactions on residual RNA fragments that are unaffected or even increased by RNase A treatment, is due to incomplete RNA digestion or if they represent direct protein interactions. 293 T-REx cells were generated to express individual ADAR1 isoforms with mutations in the KKxxK motifs in all three dsRBDs (ADAR1 EAA) which are known to disrupt ADAR1 dsRNA binding (Figure
4A
) (
21
). Individual ADAR1 EAA isoforms were tagged with Strep II-HA or BirA* at the N-terminus, allowing for the identification of HCIs following Strep-tag IP and also the identification of the proximal protein networks with BioID. Introducing ADAR1 EAA mutations resulted in complete losses of stable interactions for both isoforms, regardless of the data acquisition technique used (DDA or DIA) (Figure
4B
). However, using BioID, ADAR1 EAA mutants had a slightly higher number of high-confidence proximal proteins, especially the ADAR1p110 isoform (Figure
4B
). ADAR1 levels were determined by immunoblots for the respective epitope tags; after Strep-tag IP (
Supplementary Figure S1C
) or BioID (
Supplementary Figure S2A
) the level of ADAR1 EAA mutant isoforms were slightly lower than WT ADARs. This trend was also observed for ADAR1 spectral counts and with the Strep-tag DDA method, but not for BioID (
Supplementary Figure S4A
). Nevertheless, the numbers of identified bound proteins were comparable between WT proteins and EAA mutants for the Strep-tag method, with more proteins in the ADAR1p110 EAA BioID dataset (
Supplementary Figure S4B
and
Supplementary Table S3
).
Further comparisons between WT and EAA revealed that only a third of ADAR1p110 EAA and half for ADAR1p150 EAA proximal proteins were also identified with WT protein (
Supplementary Figure S4C
and
Supplementary Table S3
). In addition, a high number of proteins were proximal to EAA only. This suggests significant changes in the proximal protein network due to the point mutations in dsRBDs. The consequences of mutations are evident in subtle changes in subcellular localization as observed by immunofluorescence for both the Strep-tagged (
Supplementary Figure S4D
) and BirA*-tagged (
Supplementary Figure S4E
) ADAR1 EAA isoforms, which supports the BioID results. The effect of the mutation is more prominent for the ADAR1p110 EAA isoform, as indicated by the more widespread localization in the nucleus and thus higher number of novel proximal proteins. The effect of ADAR1p110 EAA on individual interactors is depicted in a protein network (
Supplementary Figure S4F
), emphasizing the dominant effect this mutation has on the ADAR1p110 isoform. In contrast the protein network of ADAR1p150 EAA reveals that few proteins are affected (
Supplementary Figure S4G
). Only a few components of SGs and PBs in unstressed conditions are affected by the ADAR1 EAA mutations, while a comparison to temporal mRBP clusters classified in this study (
54
) revealed that half of the components from each cluster were lost with ADARp150 EAA (
Supplementary Figure S4H
). The ADAR1p110 EAA mutant displayed a different pattern as it affected the majority of components from overlapping clusters.
We compared HCIs of endogenous ADAR1 in A549 cell after RNase A treatment (Figure
3
) with the those identified with Strep-tag and BioID. This comparison was performed for both ADAR1 isoforms to compare the effects of RNase A digestion and ADAR1 EAA mutation (Figure
4C
and
D
). The majority of HCIs that are not present or decreased following RNase A treatment are nucleolar proteins and are identified with ADAR1 WT and not with ADAR1 EAA, except for PDCD11 and DDX24, which validates the different methods used to identify this set of interactions (Figure
4C
). The second group of interactions affected by RNase A digestion is predominantly found with the ADAR1p150 isoform, where the majority of proximal proteins are not affected by ADAR1p150 EAA, the exception being FXR1 and MTDH. This suggests that the dsRBD mutations may have a smaller effect on ADAR1p150 protein localization and thus are detected with ADAR1p150 EAA. This may indicate contributions of the catalytic deaminase domain or the Zα RNA-binding domain to ADAR1p150 EAA dsRNA-binding. With the exception of DHX9, all other HCIs unaffected by RNase A digestion were also identified as interactor or proximal proteins to ADAR1p110 and were not detected with the ADAR1p110 EAA mutant (Figure
4D
). In contrast, those identified with ADAR1p150 were found proximal to both ADAR1p150 WT and ADAR1p150 EAA mutant, with ILF3 protein being the exception.
In summary, utilizing the ADAR1 dsRNA-binding mutant (ADAR1 EAA) in combination with RNase A digestion offers a unique insight into direct protein–protein interactions and interactions bridged via dsRNA. However, our results indicate that introducing these mutations affects protein localization, and thus the proximal protein networks, especially for the ADAR1p110 isoform which provides information about the location of the protein interactions.
Comparison of ADAR1 interactome with known interactors and RNA editing regulators
The methods we used resulted in the identification of novel interactors for both isoforms of ADAR1 during homeostasis. When comparing our dataset with the BioGRID (
55
) and IntACT (
56
) databases, we identified some of the previously described ADAR1 interactors (Figure
5A
and
Supplementary Table S3
). As expected, more overlapping proteins were observed with endo-IP dataset and with ADAR1p110 particularly with DIA acquisition. Two other studies performed large scale identification of ADAR1 protein network with proximity labelling. These included BioID of nuclear ADAR1 in HeLa and M17 cell (
16
) and APEX2 of ADAR1p110 in MCF-7 cells (
17
). Comparative analysis of the proximal protein network of ADAR1p110 WT and ADAR1p150 WT reveal proximal proteins which overlap with those identified predominantly with ADAR1p110 WT, is reproducible and irrespective of cell-type (
Supplementary Figure S5A
and
Supplementary Table S3
).
