Analysis of published interactors and the effect of RNase treatment on the interactions was also performed (Figure
5B
and
Supplementary Table S3
). As previously observed, RNase A digestion prior to endo-IP gave reproducible results for NCL and HNRNPL, which are described as RNA-dependent interactors. However, this was not observed for ILF3, ILF2, STRBP and PRKRA, while STAU1 and PKR, which show variable effects in the literature, are present with endogenous ADAR1 even with RNase A digestion but were lost or not identified with the EAA mutant. This suggests the need for two different approaches to elucidate true protein–protein interactions. Moreover, several studies have investigated regulators of A-to-I editing by ADARs; however, Quinones-Valdez
et al.
(
57
) used a more global approach to understand the effect of RNA-binding proteins on ADAR editing in both K562 and HepG2 cell lines. We compared the interactors they identified with our dataset to further correlate whether the interaction may affect RNA editing (Figure
5B
,
Supplementary Figure S5B
and
Supplementary Table S3
). Interactors found to be common with the different methods, and revealed that depletion of these RBPs had a variable effect on editing in both cell lines tested. Similar to the published observation, the majority of HCIs or high-confidence proximal proteins which we identified as overlapping with the published study, have induced differential editing in <10% of the associated testable editing sites after their knockdown in K562 or HepG2 cells (
Supplementary Figure S5B
). However, a higher number of identified RBPs increased editing when depleted, suggesting that these identified interactors are novel negative regulator of ADAR1 editing activity.
Together, these findings suggest that our dataset largely aligns with the published data, but it also provides new insights into ADAR1 interactions and novel cell specific editing regulators.
Identification of novel ADAR1 interactors during IFN induction without significant changes to the core ADAR1 interactome
Cytoplasmic ADAR1p150 is regulated by IFN and this is crucial to prevent aberrant immune responses to self-dsRNA (
7
,
18
); however, currently there is no information on the ADAR1 interactome during the IFN response. To address this, we extended our analysis of the ADAR1 interactome by analyzing the late IFN response. For this purpose, A549 cells were treated with IFNα or p(I:C), which is a synthetic dsRNA that mimics a viral infection, for 30 h before co-IP with endogenous ADAR1, while 293 T-REx cells expressing individual ADAR1 isoforms were treated with IFNα for 24 h before Strep-tag IP (Figure
6A
). These treatment conditions resulted in induction of the IFN response, confirmed by increased expression of RIG-I, MDA5 and PKR proteins (
Supplementary Figure S6A
and
B
). Moreover, treatment of 293 T-REx cells with IFNα also upregulated the expression of individual endogenous retroviruses localized in the 3′UTRs of TRIM22 (MLT1C49), OAS2 (MLT1C627) and DDX60L (MLTA10) (
Supplementary Figure S6C
). Levels of endogenous ADAR1 following co-IP from A549 cells were similar between NT and as expected IFNα-treated cells expressed more ADAR1p150 after IFN treatment (
Supplementary Figure S1B
). Levels of Strep-tagged ADAR1 were comparable across all samples (
Supplementary Figure S1C
).
