Initially, we defined the proteins in our datasets as IFN-related using the following criteria: >2-fold changes in the Interferome database (
36
) and the presence of terms; IFN, immune response and viral regulation under biological processes in Uniprot. With these criteria we found that, in each method we used, a certain number of HCIs were IFN-related (Figure
6B
and
Supplementary Table S4
). However, not all identified IFN-related proteins were HCIs (
Supplementary Figure S6D
). The elevated number of IFN-related proteins in NT conditions can be attributed to the basal level of IFN response previously observed in the A549 lung cancer cells (
15
) and evidenced by the presence of the ADAR1p150 isoform in NT conditions following endogenous ADAR1 co-IP (
Supplementary Figure S1B
). To overcome this and to identify increased interactions during the IFN response, imputed and bait-normalized MS1 intensity values of identified interactors were used for comparison between IFN-treated and NT conditions. HCIs enriched over NT conditions (log
2
FC higher than one) were predominantly IFN-related (Figure
6C
and
Supplementary Table S4
). Three independent endogenous ADAR1 co-IP experiments two with IFNα and one with p(I:C) treatment for 30 h, identified mainly regulators of the IFN response. However, of these seven HCIs, three were found to also overlap with those identified with Strep-tag co-IP in 293 T-REx. The DDA analysis identified only HERC5 as an HCI of both ADAR1 isoforms. HERC5 is a polysome-associated ISG15 ligase that modifies newly synthesized host and viral proteins during the IFN response (
58
,
59
). In agreement with our data, ADAR1 was previously described to undergo ISGylation during the IFN response, but the ISG-15 ligase has not been previously described (
60
). Two additional HCIs: HELZ2 and OAS3, were identified in 293 T-REx only with the DIA. The difference in identification of HCIs between endogenous ADAR1 co-IP from A549 cells and Strep-tag IP from HEK293T cells is probably due to the different levels of expression of IFN-related proteins observed in the two cell types (
Supplementary Figure S6E
). Interestingly, HERC5 and HELZ2 were identified as HCIs of both ADAR1 isoforms whereas OAS3 interacts only with ADAR1p150. HELZ2, which is an IFN-inducible 3′-5′ exoribonuclease and a helicase, is active on structured RNAs and has roles in regulating LINE-1 elements and in the IFN response (
61
,
62
). Moreover, both HELZ2 and HERC5 recognize LINE-1 RNA, affecting retrotransposition (
62
). Similarly, ADARs were also described to affect LINE-1 retrotransposition, however, this effect was tested in homeostasis with ectopically expressed LINE-1 and ADARs (
63
). OAS3 is a member of the 2′-5′ oligoadenylate synthetase (OAS) family activating RNase L; it requires long dsRNA for its activation, displays high-affinity towards dsRNA (
64–66
). Crosstalk between ADAR1 and the RNaseL pathway has been described in A549 cells, but the underlying mechanism of inhibition remains elusive (
67
). We utilize AlphaFold Multimer (AFM) and predicted a direct interaction between the ADAR1 deaminase domain of ADAR1 and OAS domain I of OAS3 (
Supplementary Figure S6F
). The interacting residues of ADAR1 are within the conserved dimerization helix identified as responsible for ADAR2 dimerization (
68
). This suggests a novel protein–protein interaction between ADAR1 and OAS3 as an underlying mechanism for inhibition but this requires further validation.
To elucidate if the interactions we identified during the IFN response can occur on longer RNA, RNase A digestion was performed prior to endo-IP in A549 cells, as described in Figure
3
during homeostasis. Using SAINT analysis, HCIs were further filtered for those enriched during IFN induction and for those common for all three endo-IP experiments (Figure
6D
). DTX3L is identified as a HCIs only in samples without RNase A treatment, indicating that their interaction with ADAR1 is bridged by longer RNA (Figure
6D
). While bait-normalized spectral counts of OAS3 protein are similar before and after RNase A digestion, other HCIs are slightly increased with RNase A digestion. Such interactors could occur independently of RNA substrate or be present on the shorter RNA fragments generated during RNase A digestion, as described previously in Figure
3
. Therefore, we have also performed Strep-tag IP of ADAR1 EAA mutants, dsRNA-binding mutants previously described in Figure
4
, following IFNα treatment. However, similarly to results obtained for the ADAR1 EAA mutants in homeostasis, no HCIs were identified for Strep-tag IP regardless of LC–MS/MS acquisition (DDA or DIA) (
Supplementary Figure S6G
). These results indicate that interactions of ADAR1 isoforms with HERC5, HELZ2 and OAS3 require ADAR1 to bind to dsRNA.
To summarize, ADAR1 forms previously undescribed stable interactions with IFN-related proteins during IFN induction in both A549 and 293 T-REx cells to varying levels. These interactions require ADAR1 binding to dsRNA.
A short HMW poly(I:C) treatment during the IFN response induces changes in the proximal protein network of both ADAR1 isoforms
We have shown that during an extended treatment with IFNα or p(I:C), ADAR1 forms novel interactions with regulators of the antiviral response. However, the experimental approach is restricted to stable interactions that occur during the later stages of the IFN response and in the absence of exogenous dsRNA. To address this, samples were treated with IFNα for 24 h, together with samples where cells in addition to IFNα were transfected with p(I:C) for 4 h before collection (Figure
7A
). We observed the proximity of widespread cytoplasmic staining of ADAR1p150 and G3BP1 in NT and IFNα-treated conditions (Figure
7B
). This is in agreement with our observation that ADAR1p150 is proximal to the core of the SGs in unstressed conditions, as shown in Figure
2
. The presence of p(I:C) during the IFN response resulted in G3BP1 positive foci being formed. G3BP1 is a marker for SG formation (
69
,
70
). ADAR1p150 has been previously shown to co-localize with the G3BP1 during oxidative stress and treatment with p(I:C) (
71
). We observed different patterns of co-localization of Strep II-HA-tagged ADAR1p150 WT and endogenous G3BP1 in the presence of p(I:C) during IFN response. In some foci we observed ADAR1 signal overlapping with G3BP1, and we also observe ADAR1 in close proximity to G3BP1 while we also observe ADAR1 foci that has no overlapping signal with G3BP1. This was of interest considering that various forms of cytoplasmic condensates or SG-like condensates have been described in the literature (
72
) with ADAR1 being identified as a component in some of these.
