To elucidate ADAR1 protein dynamic during IFN response and in the presence of exogenous dsRNA, we treated 293 T-REx expressing BirA*-tagged ADAR1 isoforms and GFP control and performed BioID. This treatment resulted in the induction of IFN response as observed by the increased expression of RIG-I and PKR (
Supplementary Figure S7A
). The amount of biotinylation proteins were comparable between conditions (
Supplementary Figure S2A
) while high-confidence proximal proteins were slightly higher for ADAR1p110 in the presence of p(I:C) (
Supplementary Figure S7B
).
A comparison of high-confidence proximal proteins illustrates that 57 proteins identified with ADAR1p110 and 74 with ADAR1p150 are common for all conditions, indicating that a core protein network remained unchanged with these two treatments (Figure
7C
and
Supplementary Table S4
). While 19 proteins proximal to ADAR1p150 are observed with IFNα treatment, the addition of p(I:C) resulted in most of the proteins overlapping with the IFNα treatment. In contrast, with ADAR1p110, IFNα treatment resulted in only a few uniquely identified proteins, while the addition of p(I:C) drastically increased the numbers of unique proximal proteins.
To understand the changes in the proximal protein network during IFNα and p(I:C) treatments, the individual proteins identified uniquely under these conditions are indicated by different colors on the venn diagram in Figure
7C
. These include proteins with different cellular localizations and some have known roles during the IFN response (
Supplementary Figure S7C
). We further filtered for proteins with described role in immunity as well as known ADAR1 interactors (Figure
7D
). We observed that treatment with IFNα resulted in ADAR1p110 being proximal to XPO5 and STAU1, indicating relocalization of this isoform from the nucleus to the cytoplasm. This was not observed with the ADAR1p110 EAA mutant, indicating it was dependent on ADAR1p110 dsRNA binding. The export of ADAR1p110 from the nucleus under different stress conditions has been previously described and suggested to be dependent on dsRNA-binding (
73–75
). However, it has not been known to occur during IFN induction. While XPO5 was also proximal protein with p(I:C) treatment, STAU1 was not detected under this condition. Additional proteins proximal to ADAR1p110 WT but not to the ADAR1p110 EAA mutant were unique to the p(I:C) treatment included PKR (EIF2AK2), HERC5, SSB and PRMT1 which have roles in the IFN response and TNPO1, a known ADAR1 nuclear import receptor (
74
,
76
). These may represent novel nearby proteins and interactions occurring on exogenous dsRNA which in this case is p(I:C).
As previously mentioned, the proximal protein network of ADAR1p150 was similar between IFNα alone and with the addition of p(I:C). However, a certain distinction exists between ADAR1p150 WT and ADAR1p150 EAA mutant proteins (Figure
7D
). Proteins with a role in immune responses dependent on ADAR1p150 binding to dsRNA include NFX1, SRPK2, TRIP6, IRAK1 and TRIM25. This was observed for USP10 and ZC3HAV1 during IFNα treatment and the presence of p(I:C) however as these two proteins were also proximal to ADAR1p150 EAA mutant their interaction is independent of binding to RNA. TTLL12 protein was proximal to both ADAR1p150 WT and ADAR1p150 EAA only with p(I:C). Identification of TRIM25 and ZC3HAV1 suggests that under these conditions ADAR1p150 is proximal to some of the components of antiviral SGs (
77
). TRIM25 protein has been described as an important component of these granules triggered by transfection of p(I:C) and required for the regulation of antiviral responses via ubiquitination (
77
). Considering that we did not observe more ‘drastic’ changes in ADAR1p150 proximal protein network with p(I:C), the condensation of ADAR1p150 and G3BP1 as shown in Figure
7C
, indicates the proximity of ADAR1p150 to the core of the SGs so it is readily available for formation of larger and microscopically visible condensates upon stress. Alternatively, formation of microscopically visible G3BP1 and ADAR1p150 condensates only occurs in some cells which limits the identification of proximal proteins with BioID.
Discussion
Here we employed proteomics approach to elucidate the ADAR1 interactome and proximal protein network providing a comprehensive resource for ADAR1 research. First, we performed co-IP with the endogenous ADAR1 protein from A549 cells. Next, we generated 293 T-REx cells expressing individual ADAR1 isoforms, tagging them with Strep II-HA for Strep-tag IPs to identify stable, isoform-specific, high-affinity interactions. In addition, we used BirA*-tagged ADAR1 isoforms for the BioID method to map the proximal protein network. To differentiate between proximal and distal interactions potentially mediated by RNA, we performed RNase A treatment prior to endogenous ADAR1 co-IPs and utilized the dsRNA-binding mutant (ADAR1 EAA) in the Strep-tag IPs and BioID experiments. Previously, two studies have performed large-scale approaches to explore the proximal protein network of ADAR1p110 isoform (
17
) and ADAR1 nuclear protein network during homeostasis (
16
). However, a significant gap in understanding the interactome of the ADAR1p150 isoform and the dynamics of ADAR1 interactions during stress responses, including IFN induction, remains. Therefore, to mimic the cellular conditions when ADAR1p150 is predominantly expressed, we treated cells with IFNα and mimicked viral infection with a short exposure to HWM poly(I:C). With this, we observed the dynamics in protein network of both ADAR1 isoforms under stress conditions with the BioID. Our approach identified novel ADAR1 interactors, in addition to those previously described that were dependent on both the ADAR1 isoform and the stress condition used.
Stable HCIs common to both endogenous ADAR1 in A549 cells and Strep-tagged ADAR1 isoforms expressed in 293 T-REx cells included proteins involved in rRNA biogenesis and mRNA processing. Our results are consistent with the known ADAR1 isoform subcellular localizations and biological functions (
3
,
4
,
74
). Analysis of proteins identified with Strep-tag IPs with DIA, revealed additional HCIs that overlapped with the endogenous ADAR1 co-IP. They belong to the same clusters as those identified with DDA, indicating these interactors are of lower abundance and not cell-type specific. In addition, we observed that some stable interactions identified with Strep-tag IPs were unique to ADAR1p110, which is consistent with the nuclear localization of these proteins. However, some of the HCIs were common to both isoforms, suggesting that the ADAR1p150 interactome resembles that of ADAR1p110 during homeostasis. ILF3 was identified as a common interactor and other proteins such as FXR1, G3BP2 and HNRNPM were also found to interact with both isoforms. In contrast to stable interactors, BioID proximity labeling gave distinct protein networks for both ADAR1 isoforms. In homeostasis, the ADAR1p150 cytoplasmic protein network appears to consist of weaker or more dynamic interactions, which may explain why interactions with ADAR1p150 were not identified with the stable Strep-tag IP. This proximal protein network formed a heterogeneous cluster as observed with GO-terms enrichment analysis; however, further classification revealed an overlap primarily with SG components and proteins localizing to both SGs and PBs under conditions of no stress (
52
,
53
). This suggests that ADAR1p150 associates with core SG components in the absence of external stress. Indeed, co-localization of ADAR1p150 with subset of SG markers has been previously observed during arsenate or poly(I:C) treatment in HeLa cells (
71
). Alternatively, ADAR1p150 may associate with RNPs formed in later stages of the life of an mRNA as a significant number of ADAR1p150 proximal proteins belong to these later clusters associated with translation regulation, SG and mRNA stability terms (
54
). However, SG and aged mRNA clusters are not mutually exclusive as many proteins are present in both.
The proximal protein network of ADAR1p110 identified with BioID comprises of a group of distinct proteins. They are similar to those present with endogenous ADAR1 co-IP and Strep-tag IP experiments and belong to clusters involved in ribosomal RNA (rRNA) biogenesis and processing. Spliceosomal components and certain individual proteins were observed to be unique to the BioID method. While the role of ADAR1 p110 in splicing has been investigated (
78–80
), its function in the nucleolus and rRNA biogenesis and processing, remains largely unexplored. Due to the lack of detected editing of rRNA by ADAR1, it is hypothesized that the nucleolus may serve as a storage compartment for the enzyme allowing for quick export to the nucleoplasm with the presence of the editing substrate (
3
). Moreover, this further explains subtle differences in proteins identified in both the Strep-tag and BioID datasets.
To distinguish between proximal and distal interactions mediated by RNA, we performed RNase A digestion prior to co-IP of endogenous ADAR1 from A549 cell extracts. We detected short RNA fragments remaining in the RNase digested samples which may explain the significant number of interactors that remained unchanged after RNase A treatment. The possibility exists that some interactions deemed RNA-independent may instead occur on these residual small RNA fragments, leading to the misinterpretation of them as direct protein–protein interactions. For instance, our data indicates that some dsRBPs such as ILF3, ILF2 and PKR are RNA-independent interactors following RNase A digestion. However, previous studies have found that their interactions with ADAR1 are bridged by RNA (
16
,
47
). These studies used overexpression systems, whereas our experiments utilized endogenous ADAR1 co-IPs, which may account for the discrepancies. However, interactors such as HNRNPL, NCL, and DDX54 are lost with RNase A treatment in agreement with published findings (
17
,
63
). To address the potential influence of residual RNA fragments after RNase A treatment, we used the ADAR1 EAA mutant that contains point mutations in all three dsRBDs, that abolish ADAR1-dsRNA interactions through the dsRBDs. We observed that certain interactors previously identified as RNA-independent were lost with this mutant, while others remained, consistent with the RNase A digestion results. Both ADAR1 EAA isoforms lost their high-affinity stable interactors, as determined by Strep-tag co-IPs, and this correlates with changes in their proximal protein networks observed with BioID. The effect was more pronounced for ADAR1p110 EAA due to the loss of some nucleolar interactors, which might be attributed to the observed relocalization of ADAR1p110 EAA from the nucleolus to nucleoplasm. ADAR1p150 EAA showed more subtle changes; ADAR1p150 EAA localizes to the cytoplasm exclusively, confirming the true ADAR1p150 proximal protein network and excluding associations that might occur solely due to some common nuclear localization with ADAR1p110. In summary, digestion with RNase A can give variable results likely due to incomplete digestion of dsRNA. On the other hand, ADAR1 EAA mutants that are unable to bind dsRNA give less ambiguous results; however, as their cellular localization is altered this impacts their proximal protein network.
A significant overlap of our datasets with the published interacting proteins was observed mainly for the nuclear ADAR1p110 isoform due to the lack of information on the interactome of ADAR1p150. Combining RNase A digestion and the dsRNA-binding mutant of ADAR1 illustrated the dependence on RNA of ADAR1 interactions described in the literature and classify interactions either as distal or proximal. Also, the comparison of datasets of RBPs acting as editing regulators, we observed that those present across different datasets are predominantly enriched for RBPs acting as negative regulators as their depletion results in an increase in RNA editing. For example, we identified SRSF5 which may represent a novel regulator of ADAR1 editing as it is analogous to SRSF9 which is a negative regulator of ADAR2 activity (
81
). However, observing that some interactors had no effect on ADAR1 editing activity, this could stem from editing-independent mechanism of ADAR1.
ADAR1p150 is an IFN-inducible isoform that is essential for modulating innate immune responses. Despite this, protein interactions of ADAR1p150 and of nuclear ADAR1p110 have not been fully characterized during the IFN response. To address this, we investigated the ADAR1 interactome following IFNα treatment. IFN induction led to the identification of novel ADAR1 interactors without altering core interactomes. These novel interactors are involved in regulating IFN responses and remained unaffected by RNase A treatment, suggesting direct proximal protein–protein interactions rather than being present together on long RNA. However, these interactions still require ADAR1 dsRNA-binding as they were lost with ADAR1 EAA. Among the identified interactors during the IFN response HERC5, HELZ2 and OAS3 which emerged as definite interactors as they occur in A549 and 293 T-REx cells. Identification of HERC5, an E3 ISG-15 ligase is in agreement with the report of ISGylation of ADAR1 during the IFN response (
60
). The second interactor, HELZ2, is an IFN-inducible 3′-5′ exoribonuclease that degrades structured RNA and affects the IFN response (
61
,
62
). Moreover, both HERC5 and HELZ2 have been reported to bind LINE-1 RNA and inhibit LINE-1 retrotransposition (
62
). This suggests that their interaction with ADAR1 may occur on LINE-1 RNA during the IFN response, potentially influencing LINE-1 activity. ADAR1 has been reported to regulate LINE-1 retrotransposition under homeostatic conditions with overexpressed components but the mechanism still remains unclear (
63
). The control of LINE-1 is critical, as higher RNA levels can enhance the IFN response further (
82–86
). Our findings suggest that ADAR1 may influence LINE-1 activity during the IFN response through interactions with HERC5 and HELZ2. However, these proteins also have functions outside of the LINE-1 life cycle as HELZ2 binds to structured RNA, so the interactions with ADAR1 may occur on these RNAs. A third definite interactor we identified was OAS3, a member of the OAS family that activates RNase L upon sensing dsRNA (
65
,
66
). ADAR1 has been reported to play a role in directly modulating the OAS/RNase L pathway in cultured cells (
67
). OAS3 has not been identified as a LINE-1 interactor or regulator (
62
), suggesting that the interaction with ADAR1 may occur on a different endogenous dsRNA. So, the question remains what are the endogenous dsRNAs on which ADAR1 interacts with these other proteins after IFN induction?
The interactions described above were identified during the later stages of IFN treatment when the levels of IFN-induced proteins were highest and they represent stable interactions. To gain insight into the temporal and spatial dynamics of ADAR1 during the IFN response and the consequences of exogenous dsRNA, cells were exposed to a short treatment with p(I:C) after IFN treatment, mimicking a viral infection. This resulted in G3BP1 condensates forming in some cells, which is indicative of SG assembly. The presence of p(I:C) resulted in changes primarily in the proximal protein network of ADAR1p110 which were not observed with the ADAR1p110 EAA mutant, indicating that dsRNA binding is required for these associations. We observed some proximal proteins which indicate the relocation of ADAR1p110 to the cytoplasm. Previously, the transport of ADAR1p110 to the cytoplasm was reported to occurred during ultraviolet stress; ADAR1p110 modulates apoptosis by competing with STAU1 for substrate occupancy (
75
). Our data suggest that a closer interaction between ADAR1p110 and STAU1 occurs during the IFN response. We also found that ADAR1p110 treated with p(I:C) and IFN, is associated closely with PRMT1, HERC5, and PKR (EIF2AK2). PRMT1 may serve as a novel post-translational regulator of ADAR1, as methylation sites have been predicted at Arg1090 in the deaminase domain and at the N-terminus of ADAR1 (
87
). While it is primarily ADAR1p150 that inhibits PKR activation (
13
,
14
) and we identified an interaction between ADAR1p150 and PKR during poly(I:C) treatment, its classification as an HCI was limited due to detection in only two out of three biological replicates. The interaction between ADAR1p110 and PKR is supported with reports that both ADAR1 isoforms can interact with PKR (
14
).
ADAR1p150 exhibited less pronounced changes than ADAR1p110 in its proximal protein network during p(I:C) and IFN treatments as it retains its cytoplasmic localization (
88
). Following poly(I:C) transfection, additional proteins such as TRIM25 and ZC3HAV1 were identified as proximal to ADAR1p150. TRIM25 is an RNA-binding protein that is essential for the innate immune response as it ubiquitinates various sensors (
77
,
89
,
90
). The loss of TRIM25 interaction with ADAR1p150 EAA suggests that ADAR1 proximity is dsRNA-dependent. ZC3HAV1 inhibits various viruses by binding viral RNA and promoting translational repression (
88
); its localization to SG is essential for its antiviral function (
91
). These proteins are known to localize to SG formed during viral infection or poly(I:C) treatment, which are named antiviral SGs (
77
). This implies that poly(I:C) may promote ADAR1p150 association with SG-like granules, which are distinct from those that form as a response to oxidative stress. Various granules that are distinct from SGs have also been reported, including RNase L-dependent bodies (
92
), dsRNA-induced condensates of antiviral proteins (
93
) and antiviral SGs (
77
,
94
). Our results indicate that ADAR1p150 association with SG-like granules may depend on the type of stress or RNA present.
Our study demonstrates that the network of protein interactions involving different ADAR1 isoforms is complex, multilayered and dynamic under both homeostatic conditions and during the IFN response. These findings open multiple avenues for further research, which will not only clarify how ADAR1 regulates the IFN response but also how ADAR1 itself is regulated during this response.
